{"name":"Wharf View","hex":"#65737e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wharf","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An artificial landing place for ships on a riverbank or shore.","Any bank of a river or shore of a sea."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To secure by a wharf.","To place on a wharf."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wharf"},{"term":"view","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Visual perception.\nThe act of seeing or looking at something.","The act of seeing or looking at something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To look at.","To regard in a stated way."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/view"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wharf","blocks":[{"text":"A wharf, quay, staith, or staithe is a structure on the shore of a harbor or on the bank of a river or canal where ships may dock to load and unload cargo or passengers. Such a structure includes one or more berths, and may also include piers, warehouses, or other facilities necessary for handling the ships. Wharves are often considered to be a series of docks at which boats are stationed. A marginal wharf is connected to the shore along its full length."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Houston_Ship_Channel_Barbours_Cut.jpg/330px-Houston_Ship_Channel_Barbours_Cut.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wharf","wikidata":"Q828909"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Wharf_Quay.jpg/500px-Wharf_Quay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Wharf_Quay.jpg/1280px-Wharf_Quay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Wharf_Quay.jpg/500px-Wharf_Quay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content